From 43450f9d593acfa6acc95ad9f338703c4109af42 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Leonardo Tada Antunes <leonardotada@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:07:53 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] gc: the hard limit during the mark follows how much the heap
 grew in the last marks

A thread that allocates past the hard limit while the mark runs waits for
the mark. With a heap of 1 GiB and 80M pointers the mark takes 1-2 s and
the heap grows ~280 MB meanwhile, more than the fixed quarter of the
target: 1-2 s stalls, one per cycle. The allowance is now 1.5x the largest
growth of the last marks (extrapolated to the whole mark when a thread had
to wait; decays 25% a cycle), between a quarter and half the target; a
program that needs more than half is overloading the mark and keeps the
quarter. SYMGC_ADAPTIVE_LIMIT=0 turns it off. Trace: gcwait, gcgrowth,
gcworklist, per-round used pages.

entity_init 1 GiB, 115 MB/s: stalls 10 -> 5-6, worst 1.55-1.95 s -> 0.9-1.0 s,
RSS +5-10%. At 11 MB/s frames over 5 ms 33 -> 3. Existing models unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012VbUx1Wv1w4QgcvdgLK8qz
